`$` is the general directive marker. This page owns template-specific directive
semantics; general ownership, context, argument and diagnostic rules live in
@../directives (Directives).

Template-head directives modify their containing template, regardless of whether
the head also has ordinary expressions. Their syntax does not change the placement
rules for following-construct source modifiers.

Every template directive is compiler-owned and available under every builder. There is no
builder extension point, so availability never depends on the selected builder:

- the compiler defines the directive set
- a builder contributes root-slot destinations through direct const `$in` slots, which are not
  directives
- an unknown directive is a syntax or rule error

### Frontend directives

`$before value` and `$after value`:

- add a prefix and suffix to each direct child; either directive may be omitted
- each may appear once per head, so a repeated `$before` or `$after` is rejected
- accept a compile-time String or constant template wrapper and reject runtime-dependent values
- values may contain named slots, but not default or positional slots; child insertion is implicit
- produce no wrapper for structurally absent output, but wrap a selected empty String
- wrap a direct child loop's aggregate output once

`$fresh`:

- opts one child out of the immediate parent's before/after wrapper

`$slot`:

- declares `$slot`, `$slot name`, `$slot 1` and an optional `= <compile-time String>` default
- must be the only meaningful item in its head and never opens a body

`$in name`:

- contributes content to one named slot
- accepts a bare name only, not a String expression or a positional target
- can appear only once per head
- a routed contribution, including an explicitly empty one, suppresses that slot occurrence's default

`$note`:

- discards its balanced body without evaluating it or producing output
- is the sole template comment directive, including for direct entry templates

`$doc` and `$todo` annotate following source declarations or statements instead of template heads. They are invalid here. See @../directives (Directives).

`$raw`:

- preserves authored body content and whitespace without a formatter

`$md`:

- applies Moth Markdown formatting

### Builder-root inserts

On an active application entry root, a direct top-level const `$in`
is a builder contribution rather than a template-slot helper.
The selected builder registers its destination names. Only a direct root insert
routes. A nested, stored or aliased insert remains ordinary template
composition.

Builder destinations are builder-interpreted. The HTML builder registers
`title`, `description`, `lang`, `favicon`, `body_style`, `stylesheets` and
`head`. See @../constants/#const-templates (Const templates) and
@../project-structure/#entry-config (Entry metadata and builder slots).

### Formatter directives

The compiler owns these template-formatting directives. They are available under every builder:

`$html`:

- treats the body as raw HTML content

`$css`:

- applies balanced-body CSS checking and formatting

`$escape_html`:

- escapes HTML-sensitive body content

`$code "language"`:

- highlights a code body
- removes formatting-only indentation from multiline bodies using the first content line as the baseline
- preserves indentation relative to that baseline
- escapes HTML-sensitive characters in `text` mode without applying syntax classes
- suppresses nested child-template interpretation inside the code content


Formatter directives remain constrained to formatting and composition contracts;
they cannot rewrite functions, change nominal identity, mutate HIR or weaken language
safety. For the shared directive grammar and its single-operand rule, see
@../directives (Directives).

### Formatter scope

Formatting directives do not flow automatically into nested child templates.

A nested child that needs `$md`, `$raw`, `$html`, `$css`, `$escape_html` or
`$code` declares that formatter itself.

Moth template files are the exception:

- a nested template with no explicit directive defaults to `$md`
- any explicit directive overrides that default

### Combining items in one head

A template head can hold several **comma-separated** items, including expressions
and directives. Unlike source directive runs, template heads always require
commas between meaningful items. Most directives combine freely with other
items, but structural directives can require the whole head.

`$slot` and `$note` must be the only meaningful item in their head. A slot
declares where content arrives from elsewhere, while a note discards its
balanced body. Either combined with another item has nothing to act on:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    -- Error: the slot shares its head with a string
    card = [:
        <section>["prefix", $slot]</section>
    ]
]

Put the extra content in the surrounding template body instead:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    card = [:
        <section>prefix[$slot]</section>
    ]
]

Each template head can contain at most one formatter. `$md`, `$raw`, `$html`,
`$css`, `$escape_html` and `$code` each decide how the body is formatted, so
the compiler rejects a second formatter, including a repeat of the same one:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    -- Error: both directives format the body
    io.line([$md, $raw: Hello])
]

`$in name` can sit beside other items, but it can appear only once per
head. Each insert sends its body to one named slot, so use one insert template
for each slot you fill:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    [card:
        [$in title:Hello]
        [$in footer:Goodbye]
        Body content
    ]
]
